lesson art-pictures-made-of-dots v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=4 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Pictures made of dots"
  goal "Learn how Georges Seurat painted in dots, work out which dots make a colour, and find where dots and paint mix differently."

  look {
    scene 22x11 {
      dots pic scene="sun" colours=["red", "yellow"] magnify=true at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      In the 1880s the painter Georges Seurat made whole pictures from tiny dots of colour placed side
      by side. He did not mix the colours on his palette. From across the room the dots mix in your
      eye, so red and yellow dots look orange, and the colours stay bright.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice art.dots-in-the-sun count=1 seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice art.near-or-far count=1 seed=1
    practice art.dots-from-far count=2 seed=2
    level easy {
      worked art.dots-which-two t="pink" level=medium
    }
    practice art.dots-which-two count=2 seed=1
    practice art.dots-mistake count=2 hard-count=1 seed=2
    level hard {
      practice art.near-or-far count=1 seed=7
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.dots-vs-paint
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.paint-in-dots
  }
  remember {
    say "Dots side by side mix in your eye, which adds their light. Paint stirred together takes light away. So they do not always make the same colour."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Seurat lived from 1859 to 1891 and his paintings are long out of copyright, but the lesson shows
    his way of working in our own drawing rather than a copy of his picture. The machine marks the
    questions: the colour the dots make from far away is worked out by averaging their light, which
    is what the eye does, and is not the same as mixing them as paint.

    The questions go from naming the dots, to what they make, to working back from a colour to the
    dots that make it. The two-star question rests on a real
    surprise: blue and yellow paint make green, but blue and yellow dots look grey from across the
    room, because the eye adds their light instead of each paint taking some away. Seurat knew this
    and put his dots together with care. Showing a real painting of his in a book or a gallery
    afterwards is a good way to end.

    Respond by standing back together: "From here your tree looks orange, and close up it is red and
    yellow dots." Ask which two colours they would try next.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the dots in the sun, near or far and the colour from far are as written, a pink from dots is planned once in the teacher's pen before an orange and a purple, the mistakes are in a purple and an orange, and the two-star and three-star are as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the dots are in the tree, near or far and the colour from far are as written, the dots to plan make a purple and a pink, the mistake is in a pink and the second one makes way for a second near-or-far question, asked the other way round, and the two-star and three-star are as written."
  }
}
